    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking into some premature longitudinal cracking on a new asphalt section we laid in Phoenix recently. Can you give me a summary of the common causes for this kind of pavement failure, specifically looking at thermal expansion and bitumen issues?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Premature longitudinal cracking in hot climates like Phoenix is often attributed to thermal expansion mismatch and bitumen limitations. Thermal expansion can cause cracking when the asphalt contracts and expands between layers or with the underlying substrate, leading to stress and failure. Poor bitumen choice or application can also contribute, as it may not provide sufficient cohesion to resist thermal expansion. Furthermore, premature stripping can occur when pavement moisture evaporates, causing the bitumen to lose its cohesive strength.",
      "source": "target",
